+## KVMem retained owners
+
+This fork's opt-in KVMem integration can retain an exact native prefix for a
+different conversation without consuming its source. `kvmem.conversation_id`
+selects a mutable lineage; `prompt_cache_key` and `promptCacheKey` are aliases.
+Full UTF-8 keys are bounded at 256 bytes and conflicting aliases are rejected.
+No key is required for a new user Query to discover a compatible prefix. Tool
+continuation uses append and resident-only Pressure, without foreign discovery.
+
+Only sealed host/SSD KV and immutable recurrent checkpoint bytes share physical
+allocations. Each branch has its own accepted identity, checkpoint catalog,
+MeanK, selector, Query, graph feedback and mutable engine state. A failed child
+restore preserves the source. Admission uses the global physical payload
+account plus new growth, not the sum of logical bytes across all owners.
+The native `KVMEM_SHARED` counters expose those scopes separately from RSS.
+Request completion diagnostics report planned/actual cached tokens and the
+native source (`own`, `shared`, `miss`); older engines leave these fields absent.
+Solo MTP and ordinary batch decode retain their existing execution modes.
+Owners also survive solo/batch admission, yielded prefill and MTP continuation;
+continuation after decode has an empty Query span without dropping the owner.
+Prefill yielded before its new Query keeps that still-pending bounded span.
+Foreign discovery requires `--kvmem-sessions` greater than one. With one retained
+history, a different named owner starts fresh instead of taking the active
+owner's mutable lineage. Anonymous single-history behavior stays native.
+
+`CACHE_REUSE` reports the first successful native prefix-restore boundary of an
+HTTP request. Subsequent internal legs cannot inflate this value with input
+already computed by that request. It is not a measure of net compute avoided
+across all later replay. Legacy `DONE` timing fields keep their original scope.
+`KVMEM_SHARED scope_version=2` includes all native sequence archives and slot
+checkpoint references, in addition to parked histories, at request boundaries.
+Logical state references deduplicate their immutable root when reporting
+physical state bytes. The RAM/SSD payload charge includes reservations and can
+overlap cold checkpoint bytes; do not sum these counters into RSS or VRAM.
+`KVMEM_REUSE` traces the effective bounded Query, separately from the requested
+span; a long requested user span is not the amount captured for retrieval.
+Monitor own/shared/miss counters count first native observations once per HTTP
+request and reset with the worker generation. Batch-mode parked/eviction counts
+come from the global native catalog when scope version 2 is available; older
+batch workers keep unsupported fields absent.
+CUDA execution and portable CPU tests are qualified separately from HIP.
+
+## Elastic experts (opt-in, CUDA)
+
+`strata-poly --elastic-experts` lends audited prompt scratch and expert staging pages to the expert cache
+while a single Qwen35 MoE sequence decodes. It retains stable virtual addresses and the decode prefixes;
+KV, recurrent state, persistent KVMem statistics and attention score scratch stay resident. Before a larger
+evaluation or pipelined prompt read, it reclaims expert pages and restores the full workspace before launching
+dependent kernels. Cache growth is limited to 64 MiB per 16 decode calls and retains the configured VRAM reserve.
+
+The scope is CUDA VMM, one sequence, no frozen expert placement, and no `--stage-read-mib`
+override. Unsupported configurations log that the feature is inactive. It is off by default. In KVMem's local
+console, the native argument array is `["--elastic-experts"]`; remove it to restore static workspace allocation.
+Live INFO facts expose expert slots/cache MiB and mapped/full elastic workspace MiB. `STRATA_ELASTIC_TRACE=1`
+also records periodic native CUDA free-memory and CPU/GPU expert counters for controlled comparisons.
+
+Managed KVMem MTP with a verification window of 2..4 (one to three draft tokens) supports both elastic flags.
+The retained workspace includes the draft projection and the entire verification window. Drafting reserves
+KV for the full window before writing provisional rows; verification and partial acceptance keep the narrow
+workspace. Expert growth runs only after accepted state commits, once per MTP cycle, so headroom warmup counts
+accepted cycles in this mode. Ordinary multi-token prompts and reads still restore the full workspace.
+Unmanaged speculative decoding remains outside the qualified scope.
+
+`poly_kvmem_mtp_test MODEL.gguf --elastic-experts [--window 2..4] [--no-graphs] [--q8]` checks partial/full
+acceptance against serial native KV/state, exact full/narrow target logits and draft probabilities, cancellation,
+Query/replay, real expert growth and subsequent prompt admission. `--elastic-headroom-test` uses 288 accepted
+cycles with a 512 MiB borrowing ceiling and a 512 MiB physical reserve.
+
+Qualification uses `poly_elastic_test MODEL.gguf [--no-graphs]`: exact native KV/state/statistics preservation
+across map cycles, full versus narrow workspace logits with fixed expert placement, partial-admission rollback,
+real cache growth/reclaim, Pressure and bounded Query/replay. These are functional tests, not recall-quality tests.
+
+For managed KVMem requests, elastic mode trims unused KV on the first shorter request after prefix/session
+admission. It retains every row of the accepted resident view and room for the current request, rather than
+the previous request's high water. Parked sessions retain their Host-backed history. KV still grows in 4096-row
+steps with 1024 rows of lookahead. `poly_elastic_test MODEL.gguf --kv-reclaim-test` checks accepted KV, recurrent
+state, statistics and restored-session logits across immediate reclamation.
+
+On Windows CUDA, `--elastic-experts-headroom` additionally opts into bounded steady-decode borrowing and implies
+`--elastic-experts`. After 128 decode calls it can use the reserve down to 128 MiB; after 256 calls it can exceed
+the worker's DXGI local budget by at most 128 MiB, while keeping at least 512 MiB physically free according to
+NVML. A configured reserve above 256 MiB increases these margins. DXGI and NVML must identify the same CUDA
+adapter. The worker repays any signed budget excess and restores the full configured reserve before prefill
+or KV expansion. Missing telemetry or shared-memory growth above the sampled baseline by 16 MiB disables
+borrowing for the worker's remaining lifetime; ordinary elastic behavior continues. An allocation failure
+above budget gives back a 64 MiB piece and lowers the borrowing ceiling for the worker lifetime; a failure
+without enough borrowed memory to retreat disables borrowing. The failed ceiling is not repeatedly retried.
+External physical pressure shrinks the cache. Unsupported platforms use ordinary elastic behavior.
+
+Use `["--elastic-experts", "--elastic-experts-headroom"]` in the console's native arguments; remove the second
+flag to compare ordinary elastic allocation. INFO also exposes physical free memory, DXGI budget/local/shared
+usage, headroom state and mapped KV rows/MiB. `poly_elastic_test MODEL.gguf --headroom-test` exercises real decode
+growth, prefill/KV repayment and injected pressure/allocation/telemetry/shared-memory failures. Borrowing beyond
+the Windows budget is opt-in; qualify decode performance on the target machine before enabling it.
+
+`--elastic-headroom-mib N` controls the above-budget ceiling (default 128, range 0..1024 MiB), and
+`--elastic-physical-reserve-mib N` controls the physical-free floor (default 512, range 256..4096 MiB).
+Both require the headroom switch to take effect. For an aggressive target use native arguments
+`["--elastic-experts-headroom", "--elastic-headroom-mib", "512", "--elastic-physical-reserve-mib", "256"]`.
+These are ceilings/targets, not promised allocations: WDDM can reject mappings before the physical floor.
+INFO reports requested and learned ceilings, the physical reserve and allocation backoff count.
+`poly_elastic_test MODEL.gguf --aggressive-test` checks this configuration and verifies that a learned ceiling
+is retained across later decode ticks, alongside the existing repayment and accepted-state checks.
+
